The callback form of `resolve.conditions` is not accepted: a maintainer states "We don't want to accept a function for now as it's difficult to merge if other plugins were to add more conditions" [5]. Instead, import the default list from Vite — `defaultClientConditions` for client builds or `defaultServerConditions` for server builds — and spread it into your array [5]:

```js
import { defaultClientConditions } from 'vite'

export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    conditions: ['workspace', ...defaultClientConditions],
  },
})
```

This approach was confirmed to work for the reporter's use case [4].

This is a known upstream bug in Rolldown (tracked as rolldown/rolldown#9964) affecting tree-shaking in Vite 8.1.0 with rolldown >= 1.1.0 on complex module graphs [2]. The workaround is to disable the `lazyBarrel` feature in your config [2]:

```js
export default {
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      experimental: {
        lazyBarrel: false
      }
    }
  }
}
```

A user confirmed this setting resolves the issue [3]. Alternatively, upgrading to Rolldown v1.1.4 should work, since the lazyBarrel feature was temporarily disabled upstream [1].

This is a regression in Vite 8.0.13: const enums used as types in casting disappear from the bundled output, whereas in 8.0.12 they were still present as regular compiled enums [2]. It's fixed in 8.0.14 [1]. As a workaround, avoid the direct `as` part [3]:

```ts
class Foo {
  // casting causes Phase to not be in the outputted code
  #foo = ([Phase.idle] as [Phase])[0];

  test() {
    return this.#foo;
  }
}
```

Note there's also a related report that `export default Phase;` for a const enum outputs only `export default Phase;` [4].

The failure is a Rolldown CJS namespace interop issue, not specific to `backbone-relational`: a CJS namespace import snapshots `module.exports` properties before a later CJS side-effect import mutates the same export object [3]. That's why `import * as Backbone from "backbone"` followed by `import "backbone-relational"` works in Vite 7 and in Vite 8 dev mode but fails in Vite 8 production mode [2][3]. The workaround is to use a default import instead of a namespace import [1]:

```js
import Backbone from "backbone";
import "backbone-relational";

Backbone.RelationalModel.extend({});
```

The issue was closed as "wont-fix" since the new behavior is more aligned with ESM semantics [5], and the durable fix is expected upstream in Rolldown [3].

This is a reported bug in Vite 8: when calling `build()` in library mode (e.g. from a Cypress preprocessor), `resolve.alias` is ignored unless you also move or copy it under `build.rolldownOptions` [4]. The reporter notes the error is not reproduced on Vite 7 [2]. The reproduction repo is now public [3] (an earlier 404 was because it was accidentally created private [1][3]).

Workaround per the report — duplicate the alias under `rolldownOptions`:

```js
await build({
  configFile: false,
  resolve: { alias: { "@": path.resolve("./src") } },
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      resolve: { alias: { "@": path.resolve("./src") } },
    },
    outDir: path.dirname(outputPath),
    lib: { entry: filePath, formats: ['es'], fileName: () => path.basename(outputPath) },
  }
})
```

The excerpts do not contain a maintainer diagnosis or fix for this issue.

There is no built-in option yet — the request for a `maxWarnings: number` config option (inspired by eslint) is still open and labeled pending triage [3]. A maintainer prefers keeping this in a plugin for now, noting warnings in builds are common and it can be revisited if there are more requests [1].

A plugin approach works today. This version counts warnings and throws in `buildEnd`, and reportedly works even with multiple builds [5]:

```ts
function failOnWarningsPlugin(): Rolldown.Plugin {
  let warningCount = 0;
  return {
    name: 'fail-on-warnings',
    onLog(level) {
      if (level === 'warn') warningCount++;
    },
    buildEnd() {
      if (!warningCount) return;
      throw new Error(`${warningCount} warnings present`);
    },
  };
}
```

Note the caveat that `throw` will log an error stack trace, and `process.exit` is problematic in Node.js (e.g. stdio buffers not flushed) [4].

This is a known p3-minor-bug in Vite 8.0.8: in dev, `import Foo from "cjs-package"` resolves to the whole CJS wrapper object `{ default: Component, ... }` instead of the unwrapped `exports.default`, while `vite build` works correctly [4]. The pre-bundled output ends with `export default require_lib();`, which returns the entire exports object rather than unwrapping `.default` [4]. It was closed in #22232 [1], and bluwy noted it's probably related to the "consistent CommonJS interop" migration guide [3]. The reporter observed that the documented behavior—"The module.exports.__esModule value of the importee CJS module is not set to true"—was not being honored [5].

Workarounds from the report [4]:
```ts
// For packages with a named export:
import { QRCode } from "react-qr-code";
// For packages with only exports.default, use a runtime fallback.
```

This is a known compatibility issue: after upgrading Vite to `7.3.2`, `@vitejs/plugin-legacy` fails during the legacy downgrade/transformation flow, and the failure appears tied to esbuild version resolution [3]. The problem starts with `esbuild@0.27.7`, where legacy builds fail even with a simple `legacy({ targets: ['defaults', 'not IE 11'] })` config [4]. A maintainer identified it as upstream esbuild issue https://github.com/evanw/esbuild/issues/4436 [5].

Workaround: force esbuild to `0.27.4` via an override, then reinstall dependencies [3]:

```json
{
  "overrides": {
    "esbuild": "0.27.4"
  }
}
```

The issue is labeled `has workaround` and `bug: upstream` [1], and the expectation is to track the upstream fix rather than revert Vite's esbuild version [2].

This is a known Vite 8 issue where dependency optimization causes echarts internal methods to execute in the wrong order (it works in v5) [1]. A maintainer notes it appears to work with Vite 8.0.14+ [2], though another user reports it still persists after updating rolldown to 1.0.0-rc.13 [3]. As a workaround for dev, set `optimizeDeps.rolldownOptions.output.strictExecutionOrder: true` [4]:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  optimizeDeps: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      output: {
        strictExecutionOrder: true
      }
    }
  },
});
```

The issue is labeled "bug: upstream" and "has workaround" [1].

This is a known bug in Vite 8 where side-effect imports get reordered in production builds only, causing inconsistent behavior between development and production modes [3]. It was fixed in Vite 8.0.3, probably by rolldown PR #8877 [1]. A workaround reported in the thread is swapping the order of named imports so they come before the third-party import [5]:

```diff
-import Hello from "./Hello";
 import AceEditor from "react-ace";
+import Hello from "./Hello";
```

If you're on an older Vite 8 version, upgrading to 8.0.3 should resolve it [1].

This is expected behavior, not a bug. Vite's lib mode is intended for browser-oriented libraries, so `import.meta.url` is resolved for the browser even with `formats: ['cjs']` [2]. The `'node' if the format is 'cjs'` default you quoted is Rolldown's default, not Vite's — and since `platform` didn't exist in Rollup, it didn't exist in Vite v7 either [5]. To get Node-style output, set `build.rolldownOptions.platform` explicitly [2]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      platform: 'node',
    },
  },
}
```

With that, `import.meta.url` compiles to `require("url").pathToFileURL(__filename).href` instead of `{}.url` [3].

This is a TypeScript limitation, not a Vite bug: the error (TS 2769) occurs because the object literal returned from the async config function isn't inferred as a literal type. The fix is to add `as const` [2]:

```ts
export default defineConfig(async () => ({
  plugins: [react()],
  build: {
    minify: 'terser',
  },
} as const))
```

The reporter confirmed the workarounds were helpful [1].

This is a known bug (issue #21280) where an inline `using` declaration with a `[Symbol.dispose]` method compiles to an empty object, so the dispose callback is lost and never runs [5]. It was fixed by Rollup 4.53.4 [1], so upgrading Rollup (and thus Vite's bundled version) resolves it.

Until then, these patterns survive `vite build` [4]:

```typescript
// Factory function
function disposable(fn: () => void): Disposable {
  return { [Symbol.dispose]: fn }
}
using _ = disposable(() => console.log('disposed'))

// Class
class MyDisposable implements Disposable {
  [Symbol.dispose]() { console.log('disposed') }
}
using _ = new MyDisposable()

// Assign after creation
const obj: Disposable = {} as Disposable
obj[Symbol.dispose] = () => console.log('disposed')
using _ = obj

// Object.assign
using _ = Object.assign({}, {
  [Symbol.dispose]: () => console.log('disposed')
})

// IIFE
using _ = (() => ({
  [Symbol.dispose]: () => console.log('disposed')
}))()
```

This was a regression in vite-rolldown starting in 7.1.8, where comments like `/* @vite-ignore */` and `/* webpackIgnore: true */` were removed even with `minify: false` set [5]. It was fixed in Vite 8.0.10 [1]. The cause is that Rolldown's DCE single-use var inlining drops the comment; it can be solved by disabling that inlining for DCE or by mapping the comment in Oxc's DCE pass [3].

As a workaround, put the comment before the string [4]:
```js
export async function init() {
  const bar = /* @vite-ignore */ /* webpackIgnore: true */ 'some-url'.slice(0);
  return await import(
    bar
  );
}
```

Vite doesn't treat `*.php` specially, so this is expected behavior [3]. The change is likely due to PR #20391 [4], and the maintainers recommend triggering a full reload via a custom plugin [2][3]. You can work around it with this plugin [4]:

```js
plugins: [
	{
		name: 'reload-non-vite-code',
		hotUpdate(ctx) {
			if (ctx.file.endsWith(".php")) {
				ctx.server.environments.client.hot.send({
					type: 'full-reload',
				})
			}
		},
	}
]
```

Alternatively, `vite-plugin-full-reload` should work [3].

The issue is that calling `createServer` twice (once for `vite.client.config.ts`, once for `vite.server.config.ts`) causes the WebSocket/HMR server to listen twice [1]. The maintainer's guidance: with the Env API you don't need multiple Vite servers, and you can set `server.ws: false` to disable the WS server [2]. The reporter notes that in vite@2.0.0 the `EADDRINUSE` error was silently suppressed rather than shown [3]. To avoid the double listen, disable the WS server on the config used only for `ssrLoadModule`:

```js
// vite.server.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  server: { ws: false },
})
```

The Stylus package was accidentally removed by npm because a co-maintainer published unrelated malicious packages; npm/GitHub acknowledged the error, and the issue was closed after npm restored the package [1]. While it was down, `npm ci` worked if your `package-lock.json` was created before the malware release, but `npm i` did not [4]. As a workaround, you can override Stylus to point at its Git repository [5]:

```json
"pnpm": {
  "overrides": {
    "stylus": "git+https://github.com/stylus/stylus.git"
  }
}
```

This is a known type mismatch: Vite types the `importers` option from the `sass` package rather than `sass-embedded`, so a `NodePackageImporter` from `sass-embedded` fails with `Type 'NodePackageImporter' is not assignable to type 'NodePackageImporter | Importer<"async"> | FileImporter<"async">'` [1]. Using `NodePackageImporter` from `sass` instead produces `[vite:css] [sass] Error: TypeError: importer.findFileUrl is not a function` [1]. A maintainer notes the fix is likely to swap the order so Vite prefers `sass-embedded` over `sass` [2], and a contributor confirmed reversing the check fixes it [3].

Workaround from the issue [4]:

```ts
import {defineConfig} from 'vite';
import {NodePackageImporter} from 'sass-embedded';
import {NodePackageImporter as DartSassPackageImporter} from 'sass';

export default defineConfig({
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      scss: {
        api: 'modern-compiler',
        importers: [
          new NodePackageImporter() as unknown as DartSassPackageImporter,
        ],
      },
    },
  },
});
```

The error occurs because Vite does not support processing CJS modules; externalizing the module should work, but resolving by Vite plus externalizing it (returning `{ id: resolveId, external: true }` from the `resolveId` hook) does not work, likely related to #19461 [5]. As a workaround, change the virtual module to be a facade module by tweaking the plugin like this [5]:

```js
const p = {
  name: 'server-only-module',
  async resolveId(id, importer, {ssr}) {
    const prefix = 'server-only:';

    if (id.startsWith(prefix)) {
      if (ssr) return id
      return '\0empty-module';
    }
  },
  load(id) {
    if (id === '\0empty-module') return 'export {}';
    const prefix = 'server-only:';
    if (id.startsWith(prefix)) {
      return `export * from ${JSON.stringify(id.slice(prefix.length))}`
    }
  },
}
```

The reporter's setup used a plugin to import a module (JSDOM) for SSR only, and `pnpm run build && pnpm run preview` worked fine while `pnpm run dev` failed [3].

The reported bug is that when an `.scss` file is imported via `@use` into `index.scss`, editing the imported file is detected by the watcher but the CSS is not correctly injected; editing `index.scss` directly works, and re-saving `index.scss` after changing the imported file also injects correctly [1]. A `vite build` produces the correct CSS [1].

A partial workaround: rename the main style file to `.css` (e.g. `sass/index.css`) and use regular `@import` to include the `.scss` files, then import that `.css` file from your JS entry [5]:

```css
@import 'tailwindcss';

@import 'components/_simple-test.scss';
```

```javascript
import '../sass/index.css';
```

With this, changes to `.scss` files imported in the main `.css` file trigger automatic updates, but nested `@use` imports inside `.scss` files still do not trigger a CSS update unless the main scss file is updated [5]. Note that some reporters suspect the issue may involve Tailwind rather than Vite itself [2][4].

There's no built-in option to disable CSS preloading. `build.modulePreload` only disables preloading of JS chunks, and the request is to extend it to also allow disabling CSS preloading [3]. A maintainer noted Vite could add an option to disable automatic CSS loading, but wasn't sure it could be scoped to a specific import, since a single CSS chunk may be shared by multiple dynamic imports [2]. The issue was closed as stale, with the reporter pointed to issue #13952 [1]. For consistency, disabling CSS injection during DEV would also need an option [4]. As a workaround, removing the built-in import-analysis plugin works; in Vite 8 the relevant plugin is `native:import-analysis-build` [5]:

```js
{
  name: 'preload-remover',
  configResolved(config) {
    const pI = config.plugins.findIndex(
      (p) => p.name === 'native:import-analysis-build',
    );
    (config.plugins as Plugin[]).splice(pI, 1);
  },
}
```

This was an upstream Rollup bug and was fixed in rollup v4.44.0 [2]. As a workaround, set `build.rollupOptions.maxParallelFileOps: 100` [1][3]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      maxParallelFileOps: 100,
    },
  },
}
```

One user confirmed this fixed a stuck `--watch` rebuild since v6.0.0 at CSS transformation, in a lib-mode build with more than 100 input and output files [1].

This is a known limitation of Sass's modern API, which became the default in Vite 6, and the issue was closed as won't-fix [1][3]. Sass passes `@/../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions` to Vite as `node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions`, so the alias-relative path can't be resolved [4]. The workaround is to switch back to the legacy API in your Vite config [3]:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      sass: {
        api: 'legacy',
      },
    },
  },
})
```

If you use SCSS, set `css.preprocessorOptions.scss.api: 'legacy'` instead [2].

